# System Architecture
The system follows a client-server architecture:

#Frontend
- Handles UI rendering.
- Sends requests to backend APIs.
- Displays products, carts and user actions.

#Backend
- Processes the requests from frontend.
- Handles cart operations and authentication.
- Communicates with the database.

# Database
-Stores the users, products, carts and orders.
- Ensures persistent data storage.

#Frontend Implementation
The frontend was developed using:
- HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript
- React.js

Responsibilities include:
- Rendering homepage and product page.
- Handling user interactions like login, register, cart actions, etc.
- Managing navigation between pages and components.
- Communicating with backend using API requests.

 Key Frontend Features
   Landing Page/Homepage:
  - Displays featured or all available products
  - Provides navigation to login, register, and cart
  - Acts as the entry point of the application

    Product Listing Page:
   - Dynamically displays products fetched from backend.
   - Each product includes the name, price, image and "add to cart button"
   - Supports user interaction for selecting items

     Authentication Page:
   Login Page
    - Accepts user credentials (email/password)
    - Sends login request to backend
    - Stores authentication session on success
   Registration Page
    - Collects user details
    - Sends registration request to backend
    - Redirects user after successful account creation

     Shopping Cart Page:
    - Displays all selected products
    - Shows quantity and total price calculation
    - Allows to:
      Increase/decrease quantity
      Remove items
      Update dynamically when cart changes

     Checkout Page:
     - Displays final order summary
    - Confirms purchase action
    - Sends order data to backend
    - Clears cart after successful order

     Navigation System
     - Allows movement between pages without breaking application flow
     - Maintains user session across pages
 
    Frontend Logic Flow
 Application Load Flow:
 - User opens the application
 - Frontend initializes application state
 - API request is sent to backend to fetch products
 - Backend returns product data
 - Frontend renders products dynamically on homepage
 Product Interaction Flow:
 - User views product list
 - User clicks “Add to Cart”
 - Frontend sends product ID to backend via API
 - Backend updates cart in database
 - Backend sends success response
 - Frontend updates cart UI immediately
 Authentication Flow:
 - User enters login credentials
 - Frontend validates input format (basic validation)
 - Credentials sent to backend API
 - Backend checks user database
  If valid:
  - authentication token/session is generated
  - user is redirected to homepage/dashboard
   If invalid:
  - error message displayed on UI
 Cart Management Flow:
 - User adds product to cart
 - Frontend updates cart state temporarily
 - Backend stores cart update permanently
 - Frontend syncs with backend response
 - Cart page reflects updated items and total price
 Checkout Flow:
 - User navigates to checkout page
 - Frontend retrieves cart data
 - Total price is calculated
 - User confirms order
 - Frontend sends order request to backend
 - Backend creates order record
 - Cart is cleared after successful transaction
 - Confirmation message is displayed
 State Mangement Flow:
The frontend maintains application state such as:
 - Logged-in user state
 - Cart contents
 - Product list
 - Loading states
State updates occur when:
 - API responses are received
 - User performs actions (add/remove cart items)
 - Authentication status changes

Frontend-Backend Communication Model
The frontend communicates with the backend using REST API calls.
Process:
 - Frontend sends HTTP request (GET/POST/DELETE)
 - Backend processes request
 - Backend sends JSON response
 - Frontend updates UI based on response

# Backend Implementation
The backend was developed using:
- Node.js
- Express.js
- REST API Architecture
Responsibilities include:
 - Processing frontend requests
 - Managing authentication and authorization
 - Handling product-related operations
 - Managing cart functionality
 - Performing database communication
 - Processing orders and transactions
 - Returning API responses
 - Handling validation and error management

 # Backend Modules
Authentication Module
 This handles:
- User registration
- User login
- Password validation
- User verification
- Session/token generation
Authentication Logic Flow
- User submits credentials
- Backend validates request data
- Database is queried for matching user
- Password verification occurs
- Authentication response is generated
- Session/token returned to frontend
Product Management Module
 It handles:
- Fetching products
- Product storage
- Product updates
- Product deletion
- Product retrieval by ID
Product Retrieval Flow
- Frontend sends request for products
- Backend queries database
- Product records are retrieved
- JSON response is returned
- Frontend renders products dynamically
Cart Management Module
It handles:
- Adding items to cart
- Removing items
- Updating quantities
- Retrieving cart contents
Cart Logic Flow
- User selects product
- Backend receives product ID
- Cart database is updated
- Updated cart response returned
- Frontend synchronizes cart state
Order Processing Module
It handles:
- Order creation
- Order storage
- Checkout processing
- Order retrieval
Order Processing Flow
- User confirms checkout
- Backend validates cart data
- Order record is generated
- Order stored in database
- Confirmation response returned
Server-Side Request Flow
The backend processes requests through the following sequence:
- Request received from frontend
- Route identifies required operation
- Controller processes business logic
- Database operation is performed
- Response generated
- Response returned to fronten

Routing System
The routing system organizes API endpoints into different functional categories.
Typical route groups include:
- Authentication routes
- Product routes
- Cart routes
- Order routes
 Routes improve:
 - code organization
 - maintainability
 - scalability

Controller Logic
Controllers manage the application’s business logic.
Responsibilities include:
- processing requests
- validating input
- interacting with models/database
- generating responses
Controllers help separate logic from routing for cleaner architecture.

Database Interaction
The backend communicates with the database using models/schemas.
Database interactions include:
- creating records
- updating records
- deleting records
- retrieving records
These operations allow persistent data management across the application.

Error Handling System
The backend includes error handling mechanisms to improve reliability.
Examples:
- Invalid login credentials
- Missing product information
- Database connection failures
- Unauthorized access attempts
Error responses are returned to the frontend for proper user feedback.

Input Validation
Validation ensures only correct data enters the system.
Validation checks include:
- Empty fields
- Invalid email formats
- Duplicate accounts
- Invalid product requests
This helps maintain application stability and security.

API Response Structure
The backend communicates with the frontend using JSON responses.

Example successful response:
{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Product added to cart"
}
Example error response:
{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Invalid credentials"
}


Database Design
The database system stores and manages all application data permanently.
The database stores:
- User information
- Product details
- Orders
- Cart data
The database structure was designed to ensure:
- proper organization
- efficient retrieval
- data consistency
- scalability

 Database Entities
Users Entity
 It stores user related information
 Fields:
 - userId
- username
- email
- password
- role
 Responsibilities:
- Authentication
- User identification
- Access control
Product Entity
  It stores all tjhe product information
  Fields:
  - productId
  - productName
  - price
  - description
  - image
  - category
Responsibilities:
  - Product display
  - Product management
  - Product retrieval
Cart Entity
It stores the temporary shopping information
Fields:
  - cartId
  - userId
  - productId
  - quantity
Responsibilities:
  - Cart management
  - Quantity updates
  - Cart retrieval
Orders Entity
It stores the completed purchases records.
Fields:
  - orderId
  - userId
  - purchasedItems
  - totalPrice
  - orderStatus
 Responsibilities:
  - Order tracking
  - Purchase history
  - Checkout management

Database Relationships
The entities are connected through relational or referenced relationships.
Examples:
 - Users can own multiple cart items.
 - Users can place multiple orders.
 - Products can exist in multiple carts.
These relationships improve data consistency and application structure.

System Workflow
Application Initialization Workflow
  - User accesses application URL
  - Frontend application loads
  - Backend server initializes APIs
  - Database connection established
  - Product data retrieved
  - Homepage rendered dynamically
User Registration Flow
   - User enters registration details
   - Frontend validates basic input
   - Registration request sent to backend
   - Backend validates credentials
   - Database checks for duplicate account
   - User account stored
   - Success response returned
Login Flow
   - User enters login credentials
   - Credentials sent to backend API
   - Backend validates account
   - Password comparison performed
   - Authentication response generated
   - Session/token created
   - User redirected into application
Product Flow
  - Frontend requests products
  - Backend queries product database
  - Product records retrieved
  - JSON response returned
  - Frontend displays products dynamically
Cart Flow
  - User selects product
  - Product added to frontend cart state
  - API request updates backend cart
  - Database cart updated
  - Updated cart response returned
  - Cart UI refreshed
Checkout Flow
  - User opens checkout page
  - Cart data retrieved
  - Total price calculated
  - Order confirmation submitted
  - Backend creates order
  - Order stored in database
  - Cart cleared
  - Success confirmation displayed

